{:edb/db-before-t 1844 :edb/db-after-t 1845 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535157 50 #inst "2026-10-08T18:15:12.067Z" 13194139535157 true] [17592186092033 1100 "Calculate, in degrees to the nearest degree, the solutions to the equation $5\\sin x = -7\\cos x$ for $0^\\circ \\leq x < 360^\\circ$." 13194139535157 false] [17592186092033 1100 "Calculate, in degrees to the nearest degree, the solutions to the equation $5\\sin x = -7\\cos x$ for $0^\\circ \\leq x < 360^\\circ.$" 13194139535157 true] [17592186092033 1145 "We divide each term of the equation by $\\cos x$ and use the identity\n\n$$\n\\tan x = \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5\\sin x &= -7\\cos x \\\\\n\\frac{5\\sin x}{\\cos x} &= -\\frac{7\\cos x}{\\cos x} \\\\\n\\frac{5\\sin x}{\\cos x} &= -\\frac{7\\cos x}{\\cos x} \\\\\n5 \\cdot \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x} &= -7 \\\\\n5\\tan x &= -7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\tan x$:\n\n$$\n\\tan x = -\\dfrac{7}{5}\n$$\n\nThe length of the interval for $x$ is $2\\pi$, so we will likely require $2$ solutions.\n\nWe can immediately calculate the principal value $x$:\n\n$$\nx = \\arctan\\left(-\\dfrac{7}{5}\\right) \\approx -54.46\n$$\n\nThis value is outside the domain\n\n$$\n0^\\circ \\leq x < 360^\\circ\n$$\n\nTo get a value that's inside the domain, we add $180^\\circ$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= \\arctan (-\\frac{7}{5}) + 180^{∘} \\\\\n&\\approx - 54.46^{∘} + 180^{∘} \\\\\n&= 125.54^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the other solution inside the given interval, we add $180^\\circ$ to $x_1$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= x_{1} + 180^{∘} \\\\\n&\\approx 125.54^{∘} + 180^{∘} \\\\\n&= 305.54^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the solutions, rounded to the nearest degree, are\n\n$$\nx= 126^\\circ, 306^\\circ\n$$" 13194139535157 false] [17592186092033 1145 "We divide each term of the equation by $\\cos x$ and use the identity $\\tan x = \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-7\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =-\\frac{7\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\ \\frac{5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =-\\frac{7\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} \\\\ 5\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =-7 \\\\ 5\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\tan x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\tan x = -\\dfrac{7}{5}\n$$\n\nThe length of the interval for $x$ is $2\\pi$, so we will likely require $2$ solutions. \n\nWe can immediately calculate the principal value $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx = \\arctan\\left(-\\dfrac{7}{5}\\right) \\approx -54.46\n$$\n\nThis value is outside the domain $0^\\circ \\leq x < 360^\\circ.$ To get a value that's inside the domain, we add $180^\\circ\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{7}{5})+{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈-{54.46}^{∘}+{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={125.54}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the other solution inside the given interval, we add $180^\\circ$ to $x_1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}+{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈{125.54}^{∘}+{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={305.54}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the solutions, rounded to the nearest degree, are $x= 126^\\circ, 306^\\circ.$" 13194139535157 true] [17592186092034 1100 "Calculate, in degrees to the nearest degree, the solutions to the equation $3\\sin x + 12\\cos x = 0$ for $-180^\\circ <  x \\leq 180^\\circ.$" 13194139535157 true] [17592186092034 1100 "Calculate, in degrees to the nearest degree, the solutions to the equation $3\\sin x + 12\\cos x = 0$ for $-180^\\circ < x \\leq 180^\\circ$." 13194139535157 false] [17592186092034 1145 "We divide each term of the equation by $\\cos x$ and use the identity\n\n$$\n\\tan x = \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3\\sin x + 12\\cos x &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{3\\sin x}{\\cos x} + \\frac{12\\cos x}{\\cos x} &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{3\\sin x}{\\cos x} + \\frac{12\\cos x}{\\cos x} &= 0 \\\\\n3 \\cdot \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x} + 12 &= 0 \\\\\n3\\tan x + 12 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\tan x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3\\tan x &= -12 \\\\\n\\tan x &= -4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can immediately calculate the principal value $x$:\n\n$$\nx = \\arctan(-4) \\approx -75.96^\\circ\n$$\n\nSince this value lies inside the given domain, our first solution is\n\n$$\nx_1 = -75.96^\\circ\n$$\n\nTo find the other solution in the given interval, we *add* $180^\\circ$. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= x_{1} + 180^{∘} \\\\\n&\\approx - 75.96^{∘} + 180^{∘} \\\\\n&= 104.04^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the solutions, rounded to the nearest degree, are\n\n$$\nx= -76^\\circ, 104^\\circ\n$$" 13194139535157 false] [17592186092034 1145 "We divide each term of the equation by $\\cos x$ and use the identity $\\tan x = \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+12\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\frac{12\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\frac{12\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} & =0 \\\\ 3\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+12 & =0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+12 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\tan x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-12 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can immediately calculate the principal value $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx = \\arctan(-4) \\approx -75.96^\\circ\n$$\n\nSince this value lies inside the given domain, our first solution is $x_1 = -75.96^\\circ.$\n\nTo find the other solution in the given interval, we *add* $180^\\circ.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}+{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈-{75.96}^{∘}+{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={104.04}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the solutions, rounded to the nearest degree, are $x= -76^\\circ, 104^\\circ.$" 13194139535157 true] [17592186092198 1020 #edb/ref 17592186334420 13194139535157 true] [17592186092198 1020 #edb/ref 17592186334427 13194139535157 true] [17592186092198 1020 #edb/ref 17592186334439 13194139535157 true] [17592186092198 1020 #edb/ref 17592186334445 13194139535157 true] [17592186092198 1020 #edb/ref 17592186334452 13194139535157 true] [17592186092199 1020 #edb/ref 17592186334413 13194139535157 true] [17592186092199 1020 #edb/ref 17592186334433 13194139535157 true] [17592186092199 1020 #edb/ref 17592186334459 13194139535157 true] [17592186092199 1020 #edb/ref 17592186334466 13194139535157 true] [17592186092835 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535157 true] [17592186092835 1100 "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nGiven that $x_{1}$ and $x_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $3\\sin x - \\sqrt{3}\\cos x = 0$ for $- π < x \\le π$, what is the value of $x_{1} \\cdot x_{2}$?" 13194139535157 false] [17592186092835 1100 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0$ for $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" 13194139535157 true] [17592186092835 1145 "We divide both sides of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and use the identity $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} & =0 \\\\ 3\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\sqrt{3} & =0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\sqrt{3} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\sqrt{3} & =0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nTo find the other solution, we subtract $\\pi $ from the principal value. Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\pi =-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6}=-\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}.\n$$" 13194139535157 true] [17592186334413 1017 #edb/ref 17592186334414 13194139535157 true] [17592186334413 1089 "q-122673" 13194139535157 true] [17592186334413 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535157 true] [17592186334413 1100 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x\\le {180}^{∘}$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to the nearest degree?" 13194139535157 true] [17592186334413 1113 #uuid "522c4c61-4495-5931-943a-362bbbc1e1b2" 13194139535157 true] [17592186334413 1145 "We divide both sides of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and use the identity $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\frac{6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\frac{6\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-6 & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x-6 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-6 & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6)≈{80.538}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince ${80.538}^{∘}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={80.538}^{∘}.$\n\nTo find the other solution, we subtract ${180}^{∘}$ from the principal value. Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}={80.538}^{∘}-{180}^{∘}≈-{99.462}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is $80.538\\cdot (-99.462)≈-8\\,010.$" 13194139535157 true] [17592186334414 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334415 13194139535157 true] [17592186334414 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334416 13194139535157 true] [17592186334414 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334417 13194139535157 true] [17592186334414 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334418 13194139535157 true] [17592186334414 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334419 13194139535157 true] [17592186334414 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535157 true] [17592186334414 1041 "selection" 13194139535157 true] [17592186334414 1104 #edb/ref 17592186334416 13194139535157 true] [17592186334414 1110 #uuid "fac15273-df87-5b5f-9bf3-c8a54ae6ab93" 13194139535157 true] [17592186334415 1034 "-9\\,000" 13194139535157 true] [17592186334415 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535157 true] [17592186334415 1111 #uuid "6a693634-7d7f-58c5-8543-c96b389bafc1" 13194139535157 true] [17592186334416 1034 "-8\\,010" 13194139535157 true] [17592186334416 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535157 true] [17592186334416 1111 #uuid "f4079bc1-b3b2-5fcd-8b9b-0083c0657ea9" 13194139535157 true] [17592186334417 1034 "-9\\,892" 13194139535157 true] [17592186334417 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535157 true] [17592186334417 1111 #uuid "871b6304-0373-5c38-9be8-3ff814595391" 13194139535157 true] [17592186334418 1034 "-6\\,487" 13194139535157 true] [17592186334418 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535157 true] [17592186334418 1111 #uuid "4d8cfb00-f8ac-5efe-9911-f1f11307c39a" 13194139535157 true] [17592186334419 1034 "-8\\,000" 13194139535157 true] [17592186334419 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535157 true] [17592186334419 1111 #uuid "72e352ed-257b-5929-b793-ff50dc14a93f" 13194139535157 true] [17592186334420 1017 #edb/ref 17592186334421 13194139535157 true] [17592186334420 1089 "q-21607" 13194139535157 true] [17592186334420 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535157 true] [17592186334420 1100 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" 13194139535157 true] [17592186334420 1113 #uuid "7e1c09e9-6554-5dcd-aed9-01c917440de8" 13194139535157 true] [17592186334420 1145 "We divide both sides of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and use the identity $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+1 & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ lies outside the given domain, it is not a solution.\nTo generate the solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=x+\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\pi =\\f